# ============================================================
# CHAPTER ONE: THE NIGHT SHIFT
# ============================================================

=== shelf
scene: library
cast: hero, quill:thinking
checkpoint
~ Chapter One ~

Long after the Arcade Library has closed for the evening, Quill drags a tall, narrow book across the reading table with both claws. Its cover is black velvet worn thin at the corners, its clasp is shaped like a folded bat, and when she opens it, a faint smell of candle wax and beet juice drifts up from the pages.

Quill: This is the household archive of the Vellacourt family, who have lived in the same castle on the same hill for precisely three hundred years. They are vampires, which sounds dramatic, although in practice it mostly means that they sleep late, dislike garlic bread, and write a remarkable number of thank-you notes.

She turns a page, and a small classified advertisement glows on the paper: NIGHT ARCHIVIST WANTED. MUST BE SKEPTICAL, PUNCTUAL, AND COMFORTABLE WITH BATS.

Quill: An archive is not a pile of old paper; it is a crowd of witnesses, and some of them are honest, some of them are mistaken, and a few of them are simply showing off. Our job will be to tell which is which.

The page brightens, and somewhere inside the book a very large clock strikes nine.
> Apply for the night shift -> arrival

=== arrival
scene: castle-night
cast: quill, vampire:happy
clue: The tercentenary party is on Saturday at midnight, when the old belfry will be unveiled as the new Moonlight Ballroom.
You arrive at the gates of Castle Vellacourt just after sunset, and the great door opens before you can knock. Inside, a hall glows with hundreds of candles, cobwebs drape the portraits like lace curtains that nobody has the heart to remove, and a tall woman in a high-collared cape glides forward to shake your hand with cool, careful fingers.

This is Countess Ottilie Vellacourt, who is three hundred and twelve years old and who apologizes, before anything else, for the stairs.

Vampire: You are most welcome, and I do hope the hill was not too steep; I have been meaning to have it flattened since 1790. I am, I confess, a punctilious creature: I answer every letter on the night it arrives, I fold every napkin into a swan, and I have never once been late to anything in three centuries, which is why the present situation upsets me so.

She offers you a crystal glass of something deep purple. It is beet juice, and it is surprisingly delicious.

Vampire: On Saturday at midnight we celebrate our tercentenary, and we shall unveil the old belfry, which our groundskeeper has rebuilt as the Moonlight Ballroom. Someone, however, is determined to spoil it. Last Tuesday our invitations were reprinted overnight to announce the party for the thirty-first of February, a date that does not exist. On Wednesday our beet juice order was cancelled by a letter that I certainly never wrote.

Vampire: I need the archive catalogued before the party, and I need someone who believes documents rather than gossip, because this household produces a great deal of gossip.
? L.4 : Based on context, what does "punctilious" most likely mean?
+ Extremely careful about details, manners, and being on time
- Easily frightened by small or unexpected noises
- Fond of throwing large, expensive parties
- Unwilling to trust anyone outside the family
hint: Look at her examples: every letter answered that night, every napkin a swan, never once late.
next: pemberton

=== pemberton
scene: castle-night
cast: quill, bat:happy, vampire
clue: Pemberton claims that at ten o'clock on Tuesday he "heard" a large, hairy person near the printing press.
Before the Countess can say anything more, a small brown bat drops from the chandelier, performs an unnecessary loop around your head, and lands on the back of a chair with the air of a performer arriving onstage.

Vampire: This is Pemberton, who has looked after the belfry for forty years and who also writes our household newsletter.

Pemberton presses a folded sheet of lavender paper into your hands. The masthead reads THE BELFRY BULLETIN, and beneath it, in curly letters, the motto: Only the Facts, Freshly Heard.

Bat: Delighted, delighted! You will want my eyewitness account, naturally, or rather my earwitness account, since bats see with our ears. On Tuesday night at ten o'clock, while I was patrolling the corridors as I always do, I heard a shape near the printing press in the attic, and the echo, I promise you, was large, and it was distinctly hairy.

He lowers his voice to a thrilled whisper.

Bat: Now, who in this castle is large and hairy? I name no names. But the groundskeeper, Barnaby Hollins, becomes a wolf at every full moon and sheds on absolutely everything, and I simply mention that, purely as a fact.

Quill clicks her beak very quietly, which is what she does instead of rolling her eyes.
? RL.6 : What makes Pemberton an unreliable narrator in this account?
+ He builds a confident accusation from a vague echo and a hint.
- He refuses to share any details of what he heard on Tuesday.
- He admits openly that he was asleep at ten o'clock on Tuesday.
- He is too frightened of Barnaby to tell the Countess the truth.
hint: He "heard a shape" whose echo was "distinctly hairy," and then he says "I name no names." Did he?
next: archive

=== archive
scene: castle-night
cast: quill:thinking, vampire
clue: The Chronicle says the Countess "repelled" a torch mob in 1726; the parish register says she served lost villagers soup.
The archive occupies a long candlelit gallery lined with shelves, and the shelves are lined with three centuries of paper: ledgers, letters, recipes for beet soup, and a magnificent gold-edged volume titled The Authorized Chronicle of House Vellacourt, Written by Its Countess.

You open the Chronicle to the year 1726, and the Countess, reading over your shoulder, sighs with pleasure.

In the winter of 1726, a howling mob of villagers stormed the hill with blazing torches, and the Countess, single-handed and without so much as creasing her cuffs, drove them back down the hill by the force of her terrible gaze.

Beside it on the shelf, however, sits a humble brown book, the parish register of the village of Lower Mournby, whose entry for the same night reads very differently.

December the ninth: the lamplighter fell ill, and twelve of us lost our way in the snow and climbed to the castle carrying torches to see by. The lady of the castle let us in and gave us hot soup, and she was very gracious, if somewhat pale.

The Countess clears her throat and studies a cobweb with sudden interest.

Vampire: I may have written the Chronicle during a rather vain decade.
? RI.6 : Why do the Chronicle and the parish register describe 1726 so differently?
+ The Countess wrote the Chronicle to make her family look heroic.
- The parish clerk wanted to hide the fact that the village attacked.
- The two books are describing two completely different winters.
- The register was written centuries later by people who weren't there.
hint: Who wrote the Chronicle, and what does she admit about the "rather vain decade"?
next: theo

=== theo
scene: castle-night
cast: quill, kid:thinking
clue: The cancellation letter is on lavender paper, unsigned except for "O.V.", and it says the belfry "must be left as it is."
Around midnight there is a knock at the kitchen door, and you find a teenager in a muddy jacket holding a clipboard. His name is Theo Marsh, and his family's farm has supplied Castle Vellacourt with beets for four generations.

Kid: I came to ask whether the cancellation was a mistake, because nobody cancels three hundred bottles of beet juice two days before a party unless something has gone seriously wrong.

He hands you the letter that arrived at the farm on Wednesday. It is written on lavender paper, in small, cramped capitals, and it says only this:

CANCEL THE BEETS. NO PARTY NEEDS THAT MANY. THE BELFRY MUST BE LEFT AS IT IS. O.V.

Kid: My dad thought it was strange, because the Countess usually writes us two pages about the weather before she even mentions an order, and she always signs her whole name.

Quill turns the lavender sheet over in her claws.

Quill: Paper is a witness too, and this paper looks very familiar.
> Read the latest Belfry Bulletin -> bulletin
> Go down to the village festival -> festival

=== bulletin
scene: castle-night
cast: quill, bat:happy
Pemberton is delighted to supply you with the newest issue of the Bulletin, still warm from the press and printed, like the last one, on lavender paper. The headline runs across the whole front page in enormous curly type.

HAIRY MENACE STALKS CASTLE! Sources close to this columnist (this columnist) report that a hulking, shaggy, howling figure, almost certainly a werewolf, prowled the attic on Tuesday night. Readers are advised to lock their wardrobes, count their napkins, and keep a sharp ear out for the unmistakable sound of shedding.

Bat: A fine piece of journalism, if I say so myself, and I do, frequently.

Quill reads the article twice and then sets it down very gently, as though it might go off.

Quill: Notice that on Tuesday he heard a large, hairy shape, but by today's edition it has become a hulking, shaggy, howling werewolf. Nothing new has been discovered in between, and yet the creature has grown.
? RL.4 : What is the effect of words like "menace," "stalks," and "hulking"?
+ They make the unknown figure sound frightening without adding facts.
- They give precise details that prove Barnaby was in the attic.
- They create a calm, neutral tone suited to a news report.
- They show that Pemberton has started to doubt his own story.
hint: Compare his first account, a "large" echo, with these words. Did he learn anything new, or just pick scarier words?
next: festival

=== festival
scene: pumpkin-patch
cast: quill, werewolf:happy, kid
clue: The festival programme: "Tuesday: Grand Pie Judging, eight o'clock until midnight, Village Hall. Judge: Mr. B. Hollins."
The village of Lower Mournby is in the middle of Harvest Lantern Week, and its pumpkin patch glows with hundreds of carved faces grinning in the moonlight. Theo leads you between the rows to a very large, very fluffy figure in a knitted scarf, who is attempting to balance a pie on his nose.

This is Barnaby Hollins, the castle's groundskeeper, and tonight is a full moon, so he is currently a werewolf, although he is much more like an enormous golden retriever than anything you have seen in a frightening film.

Werewolf: Oh, hello! You're the new archivist! I'm building the Moonlight Ballroom up in the old belfry, you know, new floor, new staircase, and the bats are moving to a lovely cellar with central heating. Pemberton hasn't spoken to me since I measured the rafters.

When you ask about Tuesday night, Barnaby's ears droop, and Theo answers for him by pulling a crumpled festival programme from his pocket.

Tuesday: Grand Pie Judging, eight o'clock until midnight, in the Village Hall. Judge: Mr. B. Hollins, whose decision is final.

Kid: Half the village watched him taste forty-one pies that night, and he gave every single one of them a ribbon.

Werewolf: They were all good pies. And I've never even touched a printing press.
> Walk back up the hill to the castle -> ch1-sum
> Visit the belfry on the way back -> belfry

=== belfry
scene: attic
cast: quill, bat:angry
On the way back to the archive, you climb the narrow spiral stair to the top of the old belfry. Half of it is already transformed: new oak floorboards shine in the moonlight, a crystal chandelier waits in a crate, and Barnaby's tape measures and pencil stubs lie in tidy rows along the wall. The other half is exactly as it has been for centuries, dim and dusty, with hundreds of small brown bats hanging from the rafters like folded umbrellas.

Pemberton swoops down and hangs in front of your nose, upside down and very much offended.

Bat: Isn't it hideous? Chandeliers! Dancing! Do you know what a waltz sounds like to a bat? It sounds like an avalanche of teaspoons. My family has lived under this roof since before the Countess had her first gray hair, and now we are being sent to a cellar, with central heating, as if that were a compliment.

He flutters back up to the rafters before you can answer, muttering something about the acoustics.

Quill: Interesting. He has told us a great deal about what he heard on Tuesday, and almost nothing about how he feels about Saturday, until now.
> Return to the archive -> ch1-sum

=== ch1-sum
scene: castle-night
cast: quill:thinking
Back in the archive, as the candles burn down to stubs, Quill perches on a stack of ledgers and arranges what you have collected into two neat piles: the things people have said, and the things that paper can prove.

Quill: Every account is somebody's account. Pemberton's story comes from his ears and his imagination, Barnaby's story comes from Barnaby, and both of them have reasons to tell it the way they do, but a printed programme that half the village watched come true is a different sort of witness entirely.

She taps the programme with one claw.

Quill: When a claim comes from a suspect, or from someone with a grudge, we look for a source that has no stake in the matter at all.
? RL.1 : Which quotation most directly challenges Pemberton's Tuesday story?
+ "Pie Judging, eight o'clock until midnight... Judge: Mr. B. Hollins"
- "And I've never even touched a printing press."
- "the echo, I promise you, was large, and it was distinctly hairy"
- "I may have written the Chronicle during a rather vain decade."
hint: Pemberton named ten o'clock on Tuesday. Which source has no stake in the matter and says where Barnaby was?
next: quiz-ch2

=== quiz-ch2
scene: castle-night
cast: hero, quill:happy
You take a moment to gather your thoughts. Quill draws a small, neatly lettered card from beneath one wing.
Quill: The best explorers are never experts in only one thing. Consider this a brief detour before we press on.
? QUIZ : Quick challenge before the next chapter!
