# ============================================================
# CHAPTER TWO: RUMOR AND RECORD
# ============================================================

=== ch2
scene: castle-night
cast: quill, vampire:surprised, bat:surprised
checkpoint
clue: On Friday night the Countess's portrait was found hanging upside down, high on the wall of the great hall.
~ Chapter Two ~

On your second night, you arrive to find the whole household gathered in the great hall and staring upward. The grand portrait of the Countess, painted in 1724 and hung so high that the candlelight barely reaches it, is hanging perfectly straight, perfectly level, and perfectly upside down.

Vampire: I have been insulted by philosophers, by kings, and once by a very rude goose, but I have never been hung upside down in my own hall.

Pemberton swoops in a tight, excited circle beneath the painting.

Bat: Shocking! Outrageous! And, I hate to say it, rather hairy, if you take my meaning.
> Listen to Pemberton's theory -> pemberton-argues

=== pemberton-argues
scene: castle-night
cast: quill, bat:angry, vampire
Pemberton lands on the banister and delivers his theory to the room like a lawyer in a courtroom drama.

Bat: Consider the facts, Countess. The culprit is clearly someone who wants to ruin this party. Barnaby is a werewolf. Werewolves are famously wild and disorderly creatures; everyone knows it, it is in all the old stories. Therefore Barnaby is wild and disorderly, and a wild, disorderly creature has been turning our castle upside down. I rest my case, and I shall be printing it tomorrow.

The Countess turns to you with an expression of exquisite politeness that does not quite hide her worry.

Vampire: Is that sound reasoning, Archivist? I confess it sounded quite convincing when he said it loudly.
? RI.8 : What is the main flaw in Pemberton's argument?
+ It judges Barnaby by a stereotype about werewolves, not by evidence.
- It relies too heavily on the festival programme and its times.
- It admits that Barnaby has an alibi for Tuesday night.
- It uses too many measurements of the portrait and the wall.
hint: Look at "everyone knows it, it is in all the old stories." Is that evidence about Barnaby himself?
next: accuse-choice

=== accuse-choice
scene: castle-night
cast: quill:thinking, vampire, werewolf:sad
Barnaby has come up from the garden to see the portrait, and he stands in the doorway, still enormous and fluffy from the full moon, holding his scarf in both paws and looking at the floor.

Vampire: The party is tomorrow night, and I cannot have one hundred guests arriving to find the place upside down. If you are ready to name the culprit, Archivist, I would be grateful to hear it now; if not, I suppose you may have one more night.

Quill leans close to your ear.

Quill: The loudest story in this hall points at Barnaby, and the loudest story is very tempting when a countess is waiting for an answer. Ask yourself whether you have a single document that places him in the attic, or only an echo that grew teeth.
> Name Barnaby as the saboteur -> lose-rumor
> Ask for one more night to read the evidence -> press-room

=== lose-rumor
scene: castle-night
cast: quill:sad, vampire:sad, werewolf:sad
You tell the Countess that Pemberton is probably right, and that everything points to Barnaby. The hall goes very quiet, and Barnaby folds his scarf, sets it neatly on the hall table, and walks out into the moonlight without a word, his tail hanging low.

The Belfry Bulletin prints a special edition the next morning: HAIRY MENACE UNMASKED! THANKS TO OUR BRILLIANT ARCHIVIST.

Without Barnaby, the Moonlight Ballroom is never finished, and on Saturday evening every clock in the castle mysteriously jumps forward an hour, so the guests flee home believing that dawn has come. The Countess cancels the rest of the celebration with a beautifully written apology to every guest, and then, just as the real sun begins to rise, she shows you to the door with perfect, sorrowful courtesy.

Quill: We believed the story that was told most often instead of the story that was best supported, and the real saboteur is still in the castle, laughing into his wings.
end: lose Rumor Has It

=== press-room
scene: attic
cast: quill:thinking, kid
clue: In the press, the word FEBRUARY is still set in the same curly type used for the Belfry Bulletin's headlines.
The castle's printing press lives in the attic, among dusty trunks, a dress form wearing a moth-eaten cape, and a round window full of moon. Theo has come along to help, because he says that anything involving his beets is now personal.

The press is a squat iron machine with a tray of tiny metal letters beside it, and two cases of type sit on the shelf: a plain, upright typeface labeled INVITATIONS AND OFFICIAL NOTICES, and a curly, swooping one labeled BULLETIN, PRIVATE PROPERTY OF P., DO NOT TOUCH.

Still clamped in the press, where someone forgot to remove it, is a single line of type spelling FEBRUARY, and every one of its letters is curly.

Kid: So whoever reprinted the invitations didn't use the invitation letters at all.

Quill: And the lavender paper on which the cancellation letter was written is stacked right here, in a box marked BULLETIN PAPER. A good archivist notices not only what a document says but what it is made of.
? RL.1 : Which detail best links the fake invitations to the Bulletin?
+ "spelling FEBRUARY, and every one of its letters is curly"
- "a dress form wearing a moth-eaten cape"
- "anything involving his beets is now personal"
- "labeled INVITATIONS AND OFFICIAL NOTICES"
hint: Which typeface belongs to the Bulletin, and which typeface spelled the fake date?
next: portrait

=== portrait
scene: castle-night
cast: quill, vampire:thinking
clue: The dust under the portrait is undisturbed, but there are tiny claw marks along the top of its frame.
You return to the great hall, where the Countess has fetched a ladder so long that it must be carried by three footmen, and you climb up to examine the upside-down portrait by candlelight.

The floor beneath it is covered in a smooth gray blanket of dust, unmarked by any boot, paw, or ladder foot, but along the top edge of the gilded frame you find a neat row of tiny claw marks, as if something small had gripped it and hung there for a long time.

Vampire: Barnaby is a dear, but if he had climbed up here, he would have needed this very ladder, and he would have left fur on everything, and he would have knocked over at least two candelabras, because he always does.

Quill looks up at the painting, then at the claw marks, and then, thoughtfully, at the rafters, where a row of small brown shapes is hanging, also upside down, fast asleep.

Quill: Whoever rehung this picture seems to have believed that upside down is the natural way for a portrait to hang. I wonder who in this castle might think so.
> Go back down to the archive -> tidy-offer

=== tidy-offer
scene: castle-night
cast: quill:thinking, bat:happy
When you reach the archive, you find Pemberton already there, hovering above the shelves with a feather duster clutched in his feet and an expression of radiant helpfulness.

Bat: There you are! I thought I might save you some trouble. These old papers are in a shocking muddle, so I could tidy the whole archive for you while you sleep through the day: the letters, the ledgers, all those dreary little boxes in the attic. I would put everything somewhere very safe, where it would never bother anyone again.

He beams at you. Behind him, you notice that the box of lavender Bulletin paper has been moved from the attic to the top of the highest shelf, out of reach of anybody who cannot fly.

Quill: How generous. I find that people are most eager to tidy an archive at precisely the moment when it has started to say something about them.

You thank Pemberton warmly, explain that the Countess has asked you to leave every document exactly where it is until the catalogue is finished, and take the duster away from him as gently as you can.
> Compare the letters in the archive -> letters

=== letters
scene: castle-night
cast: quill:thinking, vampire
Back in the archive, you lay the Countess's real correspondence beside the cancellation letter. Her last order to the Marsh farm, written in silver ink, begins like this:

My dear Mr. Marsh, I do hope this finds you and your excellent beets in the very best of health. The evenings are drawing in so delightfully. Would it be a frightful imposition to ask for three hundred bottles of your finest juice for Saturday? I remain, with every possible gratitude, Ottilie, Countess of Vellacourt.

Next to it, the lavender letter barks in cramped capitals: CANCEL THE BEETS. NO PARTY NEEDS THAT MANY. THE BELFRY MUST BE LEFT AS IT IS. O.V.

Vampire: I have never written a sentence that short in my life. I am not sure I would know how.
? RL.4 : How does the tone of the fake letter differ from the Countess's real one?
+ It is blunt and commanding, while hers is warm and elaborately polite.
- It is cheerful and chatty, while hers is cold and businesslike.
- It is apologetic and nervous, while hers is angry and demanding.
- It is formal and flowery, just like hers, but in a different color.
hint: Compare "Would it be a frightful imposition..." with "CANCEL THE BEETS."
next: letters-after

=== letters-after
scene: castle-night
cast: quill, vampire:sad
The Countess sits down heavily, if a person made mostly of cape and dignity can be said to sit heavily, and folds her hands in her lap.

Vampire: Curly type, lavender paper, claw marks on the frame, and a letter that cares more about the belfry than about the party. I am old, Archivist, but I am not foolish, and I believe I know what all of this means. I simply do not wish to know it.

She looks up toward the rafters of the hall, and then away.

Quill: There is more paper in this castle than we have read so far. The attic trunks hold the oldest records of all, the ones nobody has opened since the family first arrived, and old papers sometimes settle new arguments.
> Search the oldest trunks in the attic -> trunks
> Review the evidence with Quill -> ch2-sum

=== trunks
scene: attic
cast: quill:surprised, kid:surprised
clue: The 1724 Charter gives the belfry "to the bats of this house, for as long as there are bats," and no Vellacourt may move them.
With Theo holding a candle, you open the oldest trunk in the attic. Beneath a layer of lavender sachets and a very surprised moth lies a rolled parchment tied with black ribbon: the Founding Charter of House Vellacourt, dated 1724, two years before the Chronicle's famous torch mob.

Most of it concerns property lines and a long dispute about a goat, but one paragraph near the bottom has been underlined, long ago, in faded brown ink.

The belfry of this house is given to the bats of this house, for as long as there are bats, in thanks for their good company; and no Vellacourt, now or hereafter, shall move them from it.

Kid: So the Countess can't actually move the bats out of the belfry, even if she wanted to.

Quill: She doesn't know that, and neither does Pemberton. A document can change an argument before anyone has finished having it, but only if someone reads it to the right person.
> Show Pemberton the charter, privately -> secret-talk
> Save the charter for the party -> ch2-sum

=== secret-talk
scene: attic
cast: quill, bat:sad
You find Pemberton alone near the round attic window, hanging from a beam and staring out at the belfry tower, where Barnaby's new floorboards gleam in the moonlight. When you unroll the charter beneath him and point to the underlined paragraph, he reads it twice, upside down and then right side up, and for once he says nothing at all.

At last he drops down to the windowsill.

Bat: Forty years I have looked after that belfry. My grandmother was born there, and my grandmother's grandmother, and the acoustics are perfect for echoes, you know, absolutely perfect. A cellar with central heating! As if a bat cared about central heating.

He folds his wings tightly around himself.

Bat: I only meant to delay the party until somebody asked us what we wanted. Nobody asked, so I kept printing things.
? RL.3 : What best explains why Pemberton sabotaged the party?
+ He wanted to stop the bats from being moved out of their belfry.
- He was jealous that Barnaby had won a ribbon at the pie judging.
- He wanted the Countess to hire him as the new night archivist.
- He disliked parties because the music hurt his sensitive ears.
hint: Remember what Barnaby said about the belfry and the bats, and what Pemberton says nobody asked.
next: secret-end

=== secret-end
scene: castle-night
cast: vampire:happy, bat:happy, werewolf:happy
You and Pemberton bring the charter to the Countess together. He confesses everything before she can unroll it, the type, the paper, the portrait, and the clocks he had been planning to tamper with on Saturday, and then he apologizes to Barnaby, who forgives him so quickly and so enthusiastically that Pemberton is nearly knocked off his perch by a wagging tail.

The Countess reads the charter by candlelight and laughs, a sound nobody in the castle has heard in about eighty years.

Vampire: My own great-grandmother promised them the belfry, and I never read past the goat.

The party goes ahead on Saturday, but the Moonlight Ballroom is moved to the long gallery, the belfry is restored to the bats with Barnaby's new floor as a dance hall for them alone, and at midnight two hundred bats perform a fly-past beneath the moon. The Belfry Bulletin prints a full correction on its front page, and the Countess offers you a permanent position as the castle's first Night Archivist, with weekends off and unlimited beet juice.
end: secret The Founding Charter

=== ch2-sum
scene: castle-night
cast: quill:thinking
Near dawn, as the sky beyond the great window turns from black to the deepest blue, Quill gathers the household's accounts into a single stack: the Chronicle, the parish register, the Bulletin, the festival programme, the two letters, and the press itself, which cannot be carried but can certainly be described.

Quill: Look at how many of these stories were told by someone protecting something. The Countess polished her family's past in the Chronicle, Pemberton dressed up an echo as a werewolf to protect his belfry, and even Barnaby defended himself in his own words, which was natural but not enough.

Quill: Meanwhile, the sources that told us the most were the dullest ones: a parish register, a festival programme, a box of paper, a line of type. Nobody wrote any of those to win an argument.

The first gray light touches the window, and the Countess, yawning politely behind her hand, retires to bed.
? RL.2 : Which theme is the story developing through its many sources?
+ A source's confidence matters less than whether it can be checked.
- Vampires and werewolves can never truly trust one another.
- Old documents are always more accurate than new ones.
- Newspapers should only be written by trained professionals.
hint: Quill contrasts the loud, protective stories with the "dull" sources nobody wrote "to win an argument."
next: quiz-ch3

=== quiz-ch3
scene: castle-night
cast: hero, quill:happy
Before you can turn the page, Quill raises one wing, unhurried and deliberate.
Quill: The story will keep. A quick challenge first; think it through rather than guessing.
? QUIZ : Sharp minds win the day. Try this one!
