# ============================================================
# CHAPTER THREE: THE PARTY
# ============================================================

=== ch3
scene: castle-night
cast: quill, vampire:happy, werewolf:happy
checkpoint
~ Chapter Three ~

On Saturday night, Castle Vellacourt is more beautiful than you would have believed possible. Theo has delivered three hundred bottles of beet juice after all, the portrait has been turned the right way up, and a hundred guests in velvet and lace are drifting through the candlelit hall: elderly vampires from across the continent, half the village of Lower Mournby, and a delegation of very well-behaved bats.

Barnaby, in a bow tie the size of a dinner plate, is standing guard beside the staircase to the new ballroom, where the unveiling will take place at midnight.

Vampire: Three hundred years, Archivist. I keep expecting something to fall on my head.

Above the chandelier, Pemberton is circling slowly, and he seems, for some reason, to be watching the clocks.
> Follow the guests up to the unveiling -> ballroom

=== ballroom
scene: castle-night
cast: quill:happy, werewolf:happy, kid:happy
At the stroke of midnight, Barnaby throws open the doors at the top of the stair, and the guests file into the Moonlight Ballroom with a long, collective sigh of admiration. The new floor gleams like dark water, the crystal chandelier scatters moonlight across the ceiling, and through the tall arched windows the whole valley lies silver and still beneath the full moon.

Theo, carrying a tray of beet juice he has clearly decided to serve personally, nudges you with his elbow.

Kid: Three hundred bottles, delivered on time, despite a mysterious letter. My dad says that if anyone asks, the Marsh family never once panicked.

Werewolf: I did. A little. In the tool shed.

The Countess raises her glass and gives a toast so long and so graceful that several of the oldest guests are moved to tears, and then the musicians begin, and the dancing begins, and for several happy hours nothing goes wrong at all. Only Quill keeps glancing up at the rafters, where one small bat is not dancing.
> Keep an eye on the clocks -> clocks

=== clocks
scene: castle-night
cast: quill:surprised, vampire:scared, bat:surprised
clue: At about half past four, every clock in the castle struck seven at once.
The dancing goes on happily for hours. Then, at about half past four by your own watch, every clock in Castle Vellacourt, from the tower bell to the tiny enamel clock in the powder room, begins to strike, and each of them strikes seven.

Bat: Dawn! The sun! Everyone to your coffins, quickly, quickly, before it is too late!

A ripple of alarm runs through the hall as a hundred vampires reach for their capes. The Countess grips your arm, and her voice, for the first time since you met her, is not entirely composed.

Vampire: Seven o'clock? Sunrise comes before seven at this time of year. Should I ring the bell and send everyone home?

On a lectern by the door lies the castle's almanac, open to this week's page, and nobody has looked at it all night.
> Ring the bell and send the guests home -> lose-dawn
> Check the almanac before anyone panics -> almanac

=== lose-dawn
scene: castle-night
cast: quill:sad, vampire:sad
You ring the great bell, and the guests leave in a polite but extremely hurried stampede of velvet, bumping their elbows on the doorframes and apologizing to one another all the way down the hill. The toast is never made, the cake is never cut, and the three-hundredth anniversary of House Vellacourt ends in a hall full of abandoned glasses of beet juice.

Only later, as the sky outside stays obstinately dark for another two hours, does anyone realize that it was nowhere near dawn at all.

The Countess, ever courteous, thanks you for your service and shows you to the door when the real sunrise finally comes.

Quill: We trusted the loudest clock in the room instead of the quiet book on the lectern, and someone was counting on exactly that.
end: lose The Early Dawn

=== almanac
scene: castle-night
cast: quill:thinking, vampire:surprised
clue: The almanac gives Saturday's sunrise as 7:12 in the morning, and the moon still shows in the window.
You lift the heavy almanac from the lectern and find the page for this week. It lists the phases of the moon, the times of the tides at the river mouth, and a neat column of sunrises.

Saturday the thirty-first: moonset 6:40 in the morning; sunrise 7:12 in the morning.

You look up at the great window, where the full moon still hangs high and silver over the hill, without a hint of gray anywhere in the sky. Your own watch says twenty-five minutes to five.

Quill: The clocks are making a claim, and the almanac and the sky are making another. One of those can be changed by anybody with a ladder, or with wings, and the other two cannot.
? RI.1 : Which evidence best shows that the clocks are wrong?
+ "sunrise 7:12 in the morning," while the moon still hangs high at 4:35
- "Dawn! The sun! Everyone to your coffins, quickly, quickly"
- "every clock in Castle Vellacourt... strikes seven"
- "Sunrise comes before seven at this time of year."
hint: Which source is printed in advance and agrees with what you can see out of the window?
