=== confession
scene: castle-night
cast: vampire:sad, bat:sad, werewolf
When you finish, the hall is silent except for the soft hiss of candles. Then Pemberton drops from the candelabra and lands on the lectern in front of the Countess, his wings drooping.

Bat: It was me. The type, the paper, the portrait, and the clocks; I flew up and moved every hand while you were all dancing. I didn't want to spoil the party, exactly, I only wanted to spoil the ballroom, because it was going to be built in our belfry, and nobody ever asked the bats.

He turns, with great difficulty, toward Barnaby.

Bat: And I am sorry, Barnaby, about the menace business. And the stalking. And the hulking. I printed a great deal about you that I had only, well, imagined.

Barnaby blinks, and then, slowly, his tail begins to wag. Quill glances at the crumpled Bulletin on the lectern, with its curly motto: Only the Facts, Freshly Heard.
? RL.6 : What makes the Bulletin's motto ironic by the end of the story?
+ Its "facts" were rumors its own writer invented to hide his sabotage.
- The Bulletin was never actually printed on the castle's press.
- Pemberton never wrote anything for the Bulletin himself.
- Every story in the Bulletin turned out to be completely true.
hint: The motto promises "Only the Facts." What has Pemberton just admitted about what he printed?
