=== verdict
scene: castle-night
cast: quill, vampire, bat:sad
The Countess sets down her pen and turns to you.

Vampire: You have catalogued my archive and my household, Archivist, and I should value your recommendation. What should become of the belfry, and of our Bulletin?

Pemberton waits on the lectern, very small. Barnaby waits by the ballroom stairs, very large. Theo waits by the door with an empty crate, pretending not to listen.

Quill: There is more than one good answer here. One answer repairs the home that started all this, and the other repairs the record that let a rumor grow. Choose the one you believe matters most.
> Recommend sharing the belfry with the bats -> win-shared
> Recommend a fact-checked Bulletin -> win-record

=== win-shared
scene: castle-night
cast: quill:happy, bat:happy, werewolf:happy
The Countess agrees at once: the bats will keep their belfry, Barnaby will move the Moonlight Ballroom to the long gallery, and the new floor he laid in the tower will become a private dance floor for the bats, with excellent acoustics for echoes.

Pemberton is so overcome that he flies three loops around the chandelier and has to be helped down by Barnaby, who catches him gently in both paws. The party goes on until moonset, and at the very end, when the guests gather at the great window, two hundred bats sweep out of the belfry in a silver ribbon against the sky.

As the pages glow and the library slowly returns around you, Quill closes the velvet book.

Quill: Most quarrels end the moment somebody asks the quiet side what it wants. It is remarkable how rarely anybody does.
end: win The Bats' Belfry

=== win-record
scene: castle-night
cast: quill:happy, vampire, bat
The Countess decrees that the Belfry Bulletin will continue, but from now on every story must name its source and be checked against a document in the archive before it is printed, and she appoints Pemberton its fact-checker as well as its writer, which he finds both humiliating and, after a week or two, rather thrilling.

The first new issue carries a front-page correction about Barnaby, a fair account of the tercentenary, and a modest notice that the Authorized Chronicle has been revised, beginning with the winter of 1726, when a lost party of villagers climbed the hill with torches and the Countess gave them soup.

Quill: A rumor can travel around a castle three times before a fact has finished putting on its boots, but a fact, once it is written down and checked, has the advantage of still being true in the morning.
end: win Only the Facts
